  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "The English Wikipedia article on **HTTP** states that the default TCP port for unencrypted HTTP is **80**.\n\n### Relevant results\n\n1. **HTTP – Wikipedia** — In HTTP implementations, TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, typically **port 80** if unencrypted and **port 443** if encrypted. Before HTTP/3, the usual transport is TCP.  \n   Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking) – Wikipedia** — By default, HTTP uses port **80** and HTTPS uses port **443**. A URL with an explicit port (for example `:8080`) overrides the default.  \n   Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers – Wikipedia** — Port **80** is assigned to Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); versions 1.x and 2 use TCP. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP. Port **443** is HTTPS.  \n   Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **Port 80 – Wikipedia** — Disambiguation page notes TCP port 80 is most often used by HTTP.  \n   Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n5. **IANA Service Name and Port Number Registry** — `http` / `www` is registered as **80/tcp** (and 80/udp), described as World Wide Web HTTP, reference RFC 9110.  \n   Source: https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers\n\n6. **Why port 80? (Super User)** — Tim Berners-Lee’s early HTTP 0.9 text assumed port 80 when none was specified; RFC 1340 (1992) listed `www` as 80/tcp for World Wide Web HTTP.  \n   Source: https://superuser.com/questions/996828/why-was-port-80-chosen-as-the-default-http-port-and-443-as-the-default-https-por\n\n7. **Stack Overflow** — The default port for HTTP is **80** and for HTTPS is **443**; the port may be omitted from a URL when it is the scheme default.  \n   Source: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/50888270/why-do-we-need-to-specify-a-port-number-while-using-http-protocol\n\n8. **Network Engineering Stack Exchange** — Port 80 is the well-known default location for HTTP servers, so browsers do not require it in the URI.  \n   Source: https://networkengineering.stackexchange.com/questions/17316/why-is-http-port-80\n\n**Bottom line:** On the English Wikipedia HTTP article, the default TCP port for ordinary (unencrypted) HTTP is **80**.",
